  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is over the West Coast with a shortwave trough \nover Kansas. Some rain showers have developed across portions of \nnorth central Kansas and far southern Nebraska as a result of \nincreased lift from the shortwave trough. Winds are increasing out \nof the south this afternoon with a strengthening surface trough to \nthe west of the area. High temperatures today will be in the upper \n60s to lower 70s. The rain showers may continue (15% to 20% chance) \ninto this evening for mainly southern portions of the forecast area \nas the shortwave trough moves eastward. Winds will weaken tonight \nwith temperatures cooling into the 40s and 50s. \n\nAn upper ridge will become more centered over the region on \nWednesday. Winds will be fairly light (generally less than 10 MPH) \nwith temperatures warming up into the 70s on Wednesday. Winds will \nincrease on Thursday afternoon (around 10 to 15 MPH) out of the \nsouth with temperatures increasing slightly (by around 1 to 3 \ndegrees) from the previous day. Low temperatures Thursday \nnight/Friday morning will be in the mid 50s to low 60s. The warming \ntrend will continue into Friday (highs expected to be in the mid 70s \nto low 80s). Winds Friday afternoon will become gusty out of the \nsouth (gusts up to 30 to 50 MPH). \n\nA cold front is expected to move into the area on Saturday as the \nupper trough from the west begins to move over the region. Rain and \nstorm chances increase (up to around 50% to 70% chance) beginning on \nSaturday as a result of this front. High temperatures behind the \ncold front on Sunday are expected to mainly be in the 50s (although \nthere is some uncertainty in the temperatures this day). \nTemperatures on Monday are expected to be similar or even slightly \ncooler than those on Sunday. Rain chances continue into Monday (up \nto 30% to 40% chance). Low temperatures behind the cold front on \nSaturday are expected to mainly be in the 40s although some areas \nmay cool into the 30s (especially across northwestern portions of \nthe forecast area).",
